  "prediction": "The Unemployment Compensation (UC) program is a joint federal-state initiative financed by payroll taxes under the Federal Unemployment Tax Act (FUTA) and State Unemployment Tax Acts (SUTA). The program aims to help mitigate economic fluctuations, particularly during recessions, by creating a reserve fund through surplus revenue during economic growth. When economic activity is robust, SUTA revenue is greater than UC expenditures, leading to state reserves within the UTF. During economic downturns, UC tax revenue falls, and UC program spending rises, injecting additional funds into the economy through increased unemployment benefits. If a state faces insufficient funds to pay UC benefits, it may borrow from the UTF, which requires repayment with interest. The FUTA tax rate is 6.0%, with a minimum net federal unemployment tax rate of 0.6% for employers in approved states. States have autonomy in administering their UC programs but are legally obligated to pay benefits during recessions. If a state fails to repay a UTF loan, it may face a federal tax increase and credit reductions. The FUA, primarily funded by excess revenue from the Extended Unemployment Compensation Account (EUCA), may borrow from other federal accounts or the U.S. Treasury to finance loans to states. Employers pay additional FUTA taxes to cover these loans. States may apply for credit reduction caps if they meet specific criteria. Interest accrues on new loans not repaid by the end of the fiscal year. States may borrow interest-free from the UTF but must repay the loans by September 30 and not make any loans in the last quarter of the calendar year. To maintain interest-free status, states must meet funding goals and have had at least one year in the past five calendar years with an Average High Cost Multiple (AHCM) of 1.0 or higher and maintain maintenance-of-tax effort. As of January 29, 2019, approximately $68.3 million in federal UTF loans to the states were outstanding.",
  "scored_prediction": "The Unemployment Compensation (UC) program is a joint federal-state initiative financed by payroll taxes under the Federal Unemployment Tax Act (FUTA) and State Unemployment Tax Acts (SUTA). The program aims to help mitigate economic fluctuations, particularly during recessions, by creating a reserve fund through surplus revenue during economic growth. When economic activity is robust, SUTA revenue is greater than UC expenditures, leading to state reserves within the UTF. During economic downturns, UC tax revenue falls, and UC program spending rises, injecting additional funds into the economy through increased unemployment benefits. If a state faces insufficient funds to pay UC benefits, it may borrow from the UTF, which requires repayment with interest. The FUTA tax rate is 6.0%, with a minimum net federal unemployment tax rate of 0.6% for employers in approved states. States have autonomy in administering their UC programs but are legally obligated to pay benefits during recessions. If a state fails to repay a UTF loan, it may face a federal tax increase and credit reductions. The FUA, primarily funded by excess revenue from the Extended Unemployment Compensation Account (EUCA), may borrow from other federal accounts or the U.S. Treasury to finance loans to states. Employers pay additional FUTA taxes to cover these loans. States may apply for credit reduction caps if they meet specific criteria. Interest accrues on new loans not repaid by the end of the fiscal year. States may borrow interest-free from the UTF but must repay the loans by September 30 and not make any loans in the last quarter of the calendar year. To maintain interest-free status, states must meet funding goals and have had at least one year in the past five calendar years with an Average High Cost Multiple (AHCM) of 1.0 or higher and maintain maintenance-of-tax effort. As of January 29, 2019, approximately $68.3 million in federal UTF loans to the states were outstanding.",
  "score": 35.254236831623096,
  "answers": [
    "Although states have a great deal of autonomy in how they establish and run their unemployment insurance programs, federal law requires states to pay Unemployment Compensation (UC) benefits promptly as provided under state law. During some recessions, current taxes and reserve balances may be insufficient to cover state obligations for UC benefits. States may borrow funds from the federal loan account within the Unemployment Trust Fund (UTF) to meet UC benefit obligations. This report summarizes how insolvent states may borrow funds from the UTF loan account to meet their UC benefit obligations. It includes the manner in which states must repay federal UTF loans. It also provides details on how the UTF loans may trigger potential interest accrual and explains the timetable for increased net Federal Unemployment Taxes Act (FUTA) taxes if the funds are not repaid promptly. Outstanding loans listed by state may be found at the Department of Labor's (DOL's) website, https://oui.doleta.gov/unemploy/budget.asp."
  ],
